{"document":{"type":"root","children":[{"type":"heading","depth":1,"id":"performance","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Performance"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"TanStack Markdown’s performance strategy is architectural: keep parsing synchronous, keep the AST simple, split entry points, and leave expensive optional work outside the core."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"current-measurements","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Current measurements"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"The generated browser bundle report records:"}]},{"type":"table","align":[null,"right","right"],"header":[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Entry"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Gzip"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Brotli"}]}],"rows":[[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"parser"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"5.2 KB"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"4.8 KB"}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"HTML renderer"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"7.0 KB"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"6.5 KB"}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"React adapter"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"7.0 KB"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"6.5 KB"}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Octane adapter"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"7.0 KB"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"6.4 KB"}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"React adapter with streaming extension"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"7.2 KB"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"6.6 KB"}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Streaming extension"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"0.3 KB"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"0.3 KB"}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"docs preset"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"2.3 KB"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"2.1 KB"}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"callouts extension"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"0.3 KB"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"0.3 KB"}]}]]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Framework runtimes are externalized from their adapters, and the highlighter measurement uses only a callback stub. Exact generated bytes live in the "},{"type":"link","href":"https://github.com/TanStack/markdown/blob/main/reports/sizes.md","children":[{"type":"text","value":"size report"}]},{"type":"text","value":"."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"The maintained benchmark records parse, pre-parsed rendering, parse-and-render, external-highlighter, and progressive AI-response paths against pinned comparison packages. Timings vary by runtime, CPU, fixture shape, and dependency version, so the generated "},{"type":"link","href":"https://github.com/TanStack/markdown/blob/main/reports/benchmarks.md","children":[{"type":"text","value":"benchmark report"}]},{"type":"text","value":" is the only source of current CPU results."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Streaming cases include unfinished TypeScript fences at 4, 16, and 64 KiB, a tilde fence, and a fence that closes after 64 KiB. Every 32-character update reparses the accumulated source. The report records total replay time and per-update latency, including p50, p95, and maximum latency while the fence is open. Separate parse-only, plain HTML, and real "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"@tanstack/highlight"},{"type":"text","value":" runs show how the costs change as code accumulates. These Node measurements exclude browser rendering and framework updates."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"The separate "},{"type":"link","href":"https://github.com/TanStack/markdown/blob/main/reports/streaming-browser.md","children":[{"type":"text","value":"streaming library comparison"}]},{"type":"text","value":" measures TanStack Markdown React, Streamdown, and streaming-markdown in headless Chrome. It keeps React roots and incremental parser state alive between chunks, checks that the growing code stays visible, and records DOM update and forced-layout latency with plain code rendering. Run it with "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"pnpm run bench:streaming"},{"type":"text","value":" after installing Chromium through "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"pnpm exec playwright install chromium"},{"type":"text","value":", or use an installed Chrome with "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"BENCH_BROWSER_CHANNEL=chrome pnpm run bench:streaming"},{"type":"text","value":"."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"use-the-narrow-entry-point","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Use the narrow entry point"}]},{"type":"code","value":"import { parseMarkdown } from '@tanstack/markdown/parser'\nimport { renderHtml } from '@tanstack/markdown/html'","lang":"ts"},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Avoid UI adapters outside their matching framework. Import individual extensions instead of the docs preset when only one capability is required."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"parse-ahead-of-rendering","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Parse ahead of rendering"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"For content that changes less often than it is viewed, parse during ingestion or the build. Cache the serializable AST and render it for each target. The measured AST renderer is several times faster than parsing and rendering together."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"keep-highlighting-external","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Keep highlighting external"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Highlighting often costs more than Markdown parsing and can bring language grammars, themes, or WASM. Run it at build time or on the server when possible. The Markdown package will never import a highlighter from its core paths."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"reproduce-the-reports","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Reproduce the reports"}]},{"type":"code","value":"pnpm run size\npnpm run bench","lang":"bash"},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Bundle budgets are also enforced in the test suite so an accidental dependency or code-size regression fails CI."}]}],"headings":[{"id":"performance","text":"Performance","level":1},{"id":"current-measurements","text":"Current measurements","level":2},{"id":"use-the-narrow-entry-point","text":"Use the narrow entry point","level":2},{"id":"parse-ahead-of-rendering","text":"Parse ahead of rendering","level":2},{"id":"keep-highlighting-external","text":"Keep highlighting external","level":2},{"id":"reproduce-the-reports","text":"Reproduce the reports","level":2}]},"frameworks":[],"downloadMarkdown":"\n# Performance\n\nTanStack Markdown’s performance strategy is architectural: keep parsing synchronous, keep the AST simple, split entry points, and leave expensive optional work outside the core.\n\n## Current measurements\n\nThe generated browser bundle report records:\n\n| Entry | Gzip | Brotli |\n| --- | ---: | ---: |\n| parser | 5.2 KB | 4.8 KB |\n| HTML renderer | 7.0 KB | 6.5 KB |\n| React adapter | 7.0 KB | 6.5 KB |\n| Octane adapter | 7.0 KB | 6.4 KB |\n| React adapter with streaming extension | 7.2 KB | 6.6 KB |\n| Streaming extension | 0.3 KB | 0.3 KB |\n| docs preset | 2.3 KB | 2.1 KB |\n| callouts extension | 0.3 KB | 0.3 KB |\n\nFramework runtimes are externalized from their adapters, and the highlighter measurement uses only a callback stub. Exact generated bytes live in the [size report](https://github.com/TanStack/markdown/blob/main/reports/sizes.md).\n\nThe maintained benchmark records parse, pre-parsed rendering, parse-and-render, external-highlighter, and progressive AI-response paths against pinned comparison packages. Timings vary by runtime, CPU, fixture shape, and dependency version, so the generated [benchmark report](https://github.com/TanStack/markdown/blob/main/reports/benchmarks.md) is the only source of current CPU results.\n\nStreaming cases include unfinished TypeScript fences at 4, 16, and 64 KiB, a tilde fence, and a fence that closes after 64 KiB. Every 32-character update reparses the accumulated source. The report records total replay time and per-update latency, including p50, p95, and maximum latency while the fence is open. Separate parse-only, plain HTML, and real `@tanstack/highlight` runs show how the costs change as code accumulates. These Node measurements exclude browser rendering and framework updates.\n\nThe separate [streaming library comparison](https://github.com/TanStack/markdown/blob/main/reports/streaming-browser.md) measures TanStack Markdown React, Streamdown, and streaming-markdown in headless Chrome. It keeps React roots and incremental parser state alive between chunks, checks that the growing code stays visible, and records DOM update and forced-layout latency with plain code rendering. Run it with `pnpm run bench:streaming` after installing Chromium through `pnpm exec playwright install chromium`, or use an installed Chrome with `BENCH_BROWSER_CHANNEL=chrome pnpm run bench:streaming`.\n\n## Use the narrow entry point\n\n```ts\nimport { parseMarkdown } from '@tanstack/markdown/parser'\nimport { renderHtml } from '@tanstack/markdown/html'\n```\n\nAvoid UI adapters outside their matching framework. Import individual extensions instead of the docs preset when only one capability is required.\n\n## Parse ahead of rendering\n\nFor content that changes less often than it is viewed, parse during ingestion or the build. Cache the serializable AST and render it for each target. The measured AST renderer is several times faster than parsing and rendering together.\n\n## Keep highlighting external\n\nHighlighting often costs more than Markdown parsing and can bring language grammars, themes, or WASM. Run it at build time or on the server when possible. The Markdown package will never import a highlighter from its core paths.\n\n## Reproduce the reports\n\n```bash\npnpm run size\npnpm run bench\n```\n\nBundle budgets are also enforced in the test suite so an accidental dependency or code-size regression fails CI.\n","originalRawMarkdown":"---\ntitle: Performance\n---\n\n# Performance\n\nTanStack Markdown’s performance strategy is architectural: keep parsing synchronous, keep the AST simple, split entry points, and leave expensive optional work outside the core.\n\n## Current measurements\n\nThe generated browser bundle report records:\n\n| Entry | Gzip | Brotli |\n| --- | ---: | ---: |\n| parser | 5.2 KB | 4.8 KB |\n| HTML renderer | 7.0 KB | 6.5 KB |\n| React adapter | 7.0 KB | 6.5 KB |\n| Octane adapter | 7.0 KB | 6.4 KB |\n| React adapter with streaming extension | 7.2 KB | 6.6 KB |\n| Streaming extension | 0.3 KB | 0.3 KB |\n| docs preset | 2.3 KB | 2.1 KB |\n| callouts extension | 0.3 KB | 0.3 KB |\n\nFramework runtimes are externalized from their adapters, and the highlighter measurement uses only a callback stub. Exact generated bytes live in the [size report](https://github.com/TanStack/markdown/blob/main/reports/sizes.md).\n\nThe maintained benchmark records parse, pre-parsed rendering, parse-and-render, external-highlighter, and progressive AI-response paths against pinned comparison packages. Timings vary by runtime, CPU, fixture shape, and dependency version, so the generated [benchmark report](https://github.com/TanStack/markdown/blob/main/reports/benchmarks.md) is the only source of current CPU results.\n\nStreaming cases include unfinished TypeScript fences at 4, 16, and 64 KiB, a tilde fence, and a fence that closes after 64 KiB. Every 32-character update reparses the accumulated source. The report records total replay time and per-update latency, including p50, p95, and maximum latency while the fence is open. Separate parse-only, plain HTML, and real `@tanstack/highlight` runs show how the costs change as code accumulates. These Node measurements exclude browser rendering and framework updates.\n\nThe separate [streaming library comparison](https://github.com/TanStack/markdown/blob/main/reports/streaming-browser.md) measures TanStack Markdown React, Streamdown, and streaming-markdown in headless Chrome. It keeps React roots and incremental parser state alive between chunks, checks that the growing code stays visible, and records DOM update and forced-layout latency with plain code rendering. Run it with `pnpm run bench:streaming` after installing Chromium through `pnpm exec playwright install chromium`, or use an installed Chrome with `BENCH_BROWSER_CHANNEL=chrome pnpm run bench:streaming`.\n\n## Use the narrow entry point\n\n```ts\nimport { parseMarkdown } from '@tanstack/markdown/parser'\nimport { renderHtml } from '@tanstack/markdown/html'\n```\n\nAvoid UI adapters outside their matching framework. Import individual extensions instead of the docs preset when only one capability is required.\n\n## Parse ahead of rendering\n\nFor content that changes less often than it is viewed, parse during ingestion or the build. Cache the serializable AST and render it for each target. The measured AST renderer is several times faster than parsing and rendering together.\n\n## Keep highlighting external\n\nHighlighting often costs more than Markdown parsing and can bring language grammars, themes, or WASM. Run it at build time or on the server when possible. The Markdown package will never import a highlighter from its core paths.\n\n## Reproduce the reports\n\n```bash\npnpm run size\npnpm run bench\n```\n\nBundle budgets are also enforced in the test suite so an accidental dependency or code-size regression fails CI.\n"}
